j['slides']=[slide('Opening',2,'Can you design a route with no wrong turn?',['Mission: follow blood through two linked circuits.','A correct route needs both destination and direction.'],'Use a finger trace or token. Explain that the diagram is a map of flow, not a scale picture of chest anatomy.'),slide('Retrieval',3,'Direction, exchange and rate',['R1. Where does blood gain oxygen from air?','R2. What does a per-minute rate include?','R3. Does a larger pressure mean the same thing as a larger volume?'],'R1 lungs. R2 amount and time. R3 no; pressure and volume are different quantities. Accept everyday pump analogies with limits.'),slide('I do',4,'Trace the full double circuit',['Body → right heart → lungs → left heart → body.','Arteries go away; veins return towards the heart.'],'Think aloud around the loop, naming pulmonary artery and pulmonary veins as the oxygen-rule exceptions. “Away/towards” defines the vessel type, not oxygen content.',visual=v1),slide('I do',3,'Explain structure using its job',['Atria receive; ventricles pump out.','The septum separates the two sides.','The left ventricle generates higher systemic pressure.'],'Trace atrium→ventricle on each side. A thicker left-ventricle wall supports greater pressure for the systemic circuit; do not claim it normally pumps a larger long-term volume than the right side.',visual=v2),slide('I do',3,'Separate the pump from the valve',['Muscle contraction changes pressure.','Valves open or close as pressure differences change.','They help prevent backflow.'],'Use forward and reverse pressure panels. Model output separately:75beats/min ×60mL/beat =4500mL/min =4.5L/min. These are constructed examples, not a target for pupils.',visual=v3),slide('We do',3,'Route engineering desk',['W1. Match the four named vessels to their routes.','Add the missing atrium→ventricle steps.'],'Body→vena cava→RA→RV→pulmonary artery→lungs→pulmonary veins→LA→LV→aorta→body. Accept abbreviations only after names are understood.',table=table(['Journey segment','Major vessel'],routes,'Schematic adult double-circulation route.')),slide('We do',2,'Pump data challenge',['W2. Calculate output forP andQ.','Check both heart rate and stroke volume.'],'P:80×55=4400mL/min=4.4L/min. Q:100×70=7000mL/min=7.0L/min. Difference2600mL/min. No exercise test or individual health judgement is made.',table=table(['Record','Beats/min','mL/beat'],pumps,'Constructed pump records; not pupil measurements.')),slide('We do',2,'Repair the wall claim',['W3. “The thicker left ventricle proves it always pumps more blood than the right.”','Replace volume with the relevant mechanism.'],'The thicker wall supports higher pressure for the body circuit. In stable circulation the two sides have matching average outputs, otherwise blood would accumulate in one circuit.',reveal='Thicker muscle supports the higher pressure needed for the systemic route; it does not imply a permanently larger output.'),slide('Hinge',3,'Which definition survives the lung route?',['Choose the accurate statement.'],'First option correct. Re-trace pulmonary vessels if pupils classify by oxygen content.',options=[option('The pulmonary artery is an artery because it carries blood away from the heart.',True,'Direction defines an artery, even when its blood is relatively oxygen-poor.'),option('Every vein must carry relatively oxygen-poor blood.',False,'Pulmonary veins return relatively oxygen-rich blood from lungs to heart.'),option('Valves supply the force that pumps blood.',False,'Heart muscle creates pressure; valves help direct flow and prevent backflow.')]),slide('You do',1,'Produce a route-and-pump brief',['Choose your response route.','Show the route, a structural reason and one calculation.'],'Use shared route/data page. Pointing with accurate spoken names is a valid access route.'),slide('You do',10,'Make every arrow explain something',['Name the vessel and direction.','Keep pressure, volume and rate distinct.'],'Supported S1–S4; standard M1–M4; stretch T1–T4. If stuck, start from lungs→left atrium and build around the route.'),slide('Review',2,'Audience checks the exception',['L1. Share one vessel claim.','Audience asks: “Would it still work for the pulmonary vessels?”'],'Pupil corrects a definition or defends an accurate away/towards explanation. Quiet teacher/TA audience is sufficient.'),slide('Review',1,'A route and a calculation need units',['A correct name tells us where blood goes.','A correct unit tells us what the number measures.'],'Model4.4L/min as an output, not heart rate. Ask pupils to add one missing unit or destination.'),slide('Exit',1,'Three useful checks',['E1. Which chamber pumps blood to the lungs?','E2. Why is the left-ventricle wall thicker?','E3. Calculate60mL/beat ×90beats/min.'],'E1 right ventricle. E2 greater pressure for systemic circuit. E3 5400mL/min=5.4L/min. Use errors to plan route versus rate re-teaching.'),slide('Exit',0,'Next useful step',['Vessel direction • chamber route • pressure • output units.'],'Shared exit minute. No comparison with a pupil’s pulse or health is required.')]
j['pupilPages']=[page('Shared evidence | Route and pump records','The route is a simplified adult circulation map. Pump numbers are constructed for learning.',[tb(['Journey segment','Major vessel'],routes),tb(['Pump record','Heart rate(beats/min)','Stroke volume(mL/beat)'],pumps),txt('Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume.1L=1000mL. Atria receive blood; ventricles pump blood out. The two sides are separated by the septum.'),response('W1. Trace the route. W2. Calculate both outputs. W3. Repair the thicker-wall claim.',4)]),page('Supported route | Follow the blood',['Use the vessel table and calculator if needed.'][0],[response('S1. Complete a full route from body back to body, including lungs and the four chambers.',5),response('S2. Explain why the pulmonary artery is an artery even though its blood is relatively oxygen-poor.',3),response('S3. Calculate output forP andQ. Give one result in both mL/min andL/min.',4),response('S4. Explain one valve action and why the left-ventricle wall is thicker.',3)]),page('Standard route | Route-and-pump explanation','Use scientific names, mechanisms and units.',[response('M1. Trace body→lungs→body through the named vessels and chambers. Explain the septum’s role.',5),response('M2. Explain how chamber-wall thickness and valves support the circulation, separating pressure from output.',4),response('M3. Calculate both constructed outputs and their difference, with units.',3),response('M4. Evaluate: “The faster pulse alone proves more blood is pumped each minute.” State what else is needed.',3)]),page('Stretch route | Test the pump interpretation','Optional transfer. A constructed recordR has120beats/min and35mL/beat. A recordS has70beats/min and70mL/beat.',[response('T1. Calculate both outputs. Which is greater? Explain why heart rate alone misleads.',4),response('T2. A faulty valve permits some backflow. Why might total chamber movement overestimate useful forward output?',4),response('T3. Explain why long-term unequal left/right outputs would be a problem for a linked circulation.',3),response('T4. A technician reports “5400beats/min output”. Correct the quantity/unit error without diagnosing a person.',3)]),page('Exit and reflection | A direction and a quantity','Keep pressure, volume and time distinct.',[response('E1. Which chamber pumps to the lungs?',2),response('E2. Explain the thicker left-ventricle wall using pressure.',3),response('E3.60mL/beat ×90beats/min: show output and units.',3),response('L1. What did your audience ask? Improve one route or calculation label.',3),choices('Next useful step',['Define vessels by direction.','Trace all four chambers.','Explain pressure and valves.','Calculate output with units.'])])]
j['answers']=[qa('Retrieval and We do','R1 lungs;R2 amount/time;R3 pressure differs from volume. W1 full route:body→vena cava→right atrium→right ventricle→pulmonary artery→lungs→pulmonary veins→left atrium→left ventricle→aorta→body. W2 P=4400mL/min;Q=7000mL/min. W3 thicker left wall supports higher pressure, not permanently larger output.'),qa('Hinge','Pulmonary artery carries blood away from heart, so it is an artery. Pulmonary veins carry relatively oxygen-rich blood towards heart. Muscle supplies pumping pressure; valves direct flow.'),qa('S1 / M1','Use the full route above. Septum separates left and right sides, limiting mixing of the two streams in the normal adult heart.'),qa('S2','An artery is defined by blood travelling away from the heart, not its oxygen content. The pulmonary artery runs from right ventricle towards lungs.'),qa('S3 / M3','P:80×55=4400mL/min=4.4L/min. Q:100×70=7000mL/min=7.0L/min. Difference2600mL/min=2.6L/min.'),qa('S4 / M2','Pressure differences open/close valve leaflets, preventing backflow. The left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall to produce the higher pressure for systemic circulation. In stable circulation average outputs of the two sides match.'),qa('M4 / T1','Need stroke volume too. R:120×35=4200mL/min;S:70×70=4900mL/min. S has lower heart rate but greater output because its stroke volume is much larger.'),qa('T2','Some moved blood returns backwards rather than progressing through the circuit; useful net forward flow can be lower than gross chamber movement.'),qa('T3','Because the circuits connect, persistently unequal average outputs would cause blood to accumulate in one part and deplete another. The thicker wall is primarily a pressure adaptation.'),qa('T4','Output is a volume per time, so5400mL/min (if those were the measured volume data), not beats/min. Heart rate uses beats/min; a unit alone cannot diagnose health.'),qa('Exit and assessment','E1 right ventricle. E2 higher systemic pressure. E3 60×90=5400mL/min=5.4L/min. Secure core:route direction, pressure/valve mechanism and valid output units. Re-teach using away/towards if pulmonary vessels are misclassified.')]
j['teacherNotes'] += ['All pump data are constructed. This lesson requires no pulse-taking, exertion, health comparison or physiological threshold.','Interactive route/valve/data decisions use the same evidence asW1–W3. Manual progression and a paper finger-trace provide equivalent reasoning.','The heart diagrams are simplified flow/structure models with text labels as well as colour; they are not diagnostic anatomical images.']
j['interactive']=interactive('Route engineering: trace, calculate, challenge','Original flow models and constructed pump records.',[step('Where does right-ventricle blood go next?','Through the pulmonary artery to the lungs.','Through the aorta to the body.',v1,'The right ventricle supplies the pulmonary circuit.','The aorta leaves the left ventricle for the systemic circuit.'),step('What is the valve’s main role in this model?','Limit reverse flow as pressure changes.','Create the pumping force.',v3,'Valves open/close with pressure differences and help prevent backflow.','Heart muscle supplies the pumping pressure.'),step('Does a thicker left wall prove permanently greater output than the right?','No: it supports higher pressure.','Yes: the two circuits need unequal outputs.',v2,'Linked circuits have matching average outputs in stable circulation.','Unequal long-term outputs would cause accumulation; wall thickness supports pressure.')])
